findings.description:
The document treats PR title, body and threads as untrusted text (Technical Considerations, "Prompt inputs from the PR"). It does not apply the same caution to the scratch worktree.

In the current code, `_resolve_review_cwd` (`src/squadron/cli/commands/review.py:244-258`) does two things with the review working directory:
- it uses it as the tool jail root;
- it passes it to `resolve_rules_dir`.

If `sq review pr` hands the scratch worktree to the existing code path as its working directory, as the Envisioned State says, then three things in the PR author's head commit steer the review:
- the rules directory;
- `CLAUDE.md`;
- `.claude/rules/*`.

A PR could weaken or rewrite the review's own conventions. "Rules outside a planned project" only covers repositories that have no rules. The architecture needs to say where rules come from in a PR review: the base ref, the operator's checkout, or the template only. It must never be the PR head. It should also say that the tool jail root (the PR files) and the rules root are separate settings.

The document says a PR-keyed name will "satisfy the review frontmatter contract without a slice index". It does not say what that contract becomes. `format_review_markdown` hard-codes:
- the `slice:` and `project:` frontmatter;
- a title of `# Review: {type} — slice {index}`;
- `"unknown"` / `0` fallbacks when there is no slice. Those are exactly the silent placeholders the project rules forbid.

`save_review_file` and `save_review_result` both build filenames from `{index}-review.{type}.{slice_name}`.

The document also claims that "archiving, digest, and integrity checks … apply unchanged" and that results land in a "configured squadron data directory". Both are unverified. `REVIEWS_DIR` is a fixed path relative to the project, and the resolve path (slice 306) finds reviews through that same constant. A review saved outside the project would be invisible to resolve and digest.

The architecture should define:
- the PR target's frontmatter fields (host, repo, number, head SHA);
- the filename scheme, including collisions across repositories in a shared data directory;
- the config key for the data directory;
- which consumers of `REVIEWS_DIR` must learn about the second location.

A PR can only be created for a branch that exists on the host. The document never says whether `sq pr create`:
- pushes the branch;
- requires it to be pushed already and refuses otherwise;
- delegates to `gh pr create`'s interactive push prompt.

A push is an outward-facing write. The "reads before writes, and writes are explicit" principle and the git rules both make it load-bearing. It needs a stated behavior and a named failure mode, such as "branch not on remote" or "local branch ahead of remote".

The Overview itself says the three capabilities "share one new boundary and nothing else". The motivation (slice 916, enterprise PR review) drives capabilities 1 and 2. `sq pr create` adds:
- a model-composition step;
- slice-artifact gathering;
- review selection;
- integration-branch targeting;
- push semantics;
- a "body section contract other tooling can rely on", which `sq review pr` is supposed to parse. No component parses it yet.

This is the heaviest slice and the least tied to the stated driver. It also carries a designed-for-not-built contract. Recommendations:
- defer `sq pr create` to its own initiative once PR review has proven the adapter;
- or ship it with deterministic assembly only (commits, linked slice, review provenance) and drop the one-shot model call;
- drop the "parseable by `sq review pr`" contract until a consumer exists.

The "updates or supersedes" posting behavior also leaves two strategies open where one is enough. Pick one. Editing the marked comment in place is the simpler choice.
